Sval: a new tab opens in its window's workspace jar

Every tab in an isolated workspace uses that workspace's cookie jar
(isolated workspaces spec §4). A tab with no opener and no userContextId
asked for gets gBrowser.defaultUserContextId, which Sval sets to the jar of
the workspace the window shows. That covers every way Firefox opens a tab
for the user, among them Cmd+T, the File menu, a favorite, history and a
link from another app, through the one function they all reach. A tab with
an opener still inherits the opener's jar, and an explicit userContextId
still wins. No event fires early enough: the browser takes its origin
attributes when the tab is created, before TabOpen.

A link from another app reaches the window through BrowserDOMWindow's
openURI with OPEN_EXTERNAL, which in Firefox asks for the default jar by id
when it guesses none. Here it asks for none, so the tab is made in the
workspace jar and its first request carries that jar. app-sval-prefs.js
turns the guess off.

